,"H413":"to(wards)","H6607":"entrance","H1004":"place","H5414":"give","H312":"another","H1935":"splendor","H8141":"year","H394":"cruel","H7646":"to satisfy","H3581":"reptile","H6089":"toil","H5237":"foreign","H5098":"to groan","H3615":"finish","H1320":"flesh","H7607":"flesh","H559":"to say","H349":"how?","H8130":"to hate","H4148":"discipline","H8433":"rebuke","H5006":"to spurn","H3820":"heart","H6963":"voice","H3384":"to shoot","H3925":"teach","H4592":"little","H1961":"to be","H3605":"all","H7451":"harmful","H8432":"midst","H6951":"assembly","H5712":"congregation","H8354":"to drink","H4325":"water","H953":"pit","H5140":"to flow","H875":"well","H6327":"to scatter","H4599":"spring","H2351":"outside","H7339":"street/plaza","H6388":"stream","H905":"pole","H369":"nothing","H854":"with","H4726":"fountain","H1288":"to bless","H8055":"to rejoice","H802":"woman","H5271":"youth","H365":"doe","H158":"lover","H3280":"doe","H2580":"favor","H1717":"breast","H7301":"to quench","H6256":"time","H160":"love","H7686":"to wander","H8548":"continually","H4100":"what?","H2263":"to embrace","H2436":"embrace","H5227":"before","H5869":"eye","H3068":"LORD","H376":"man","H5771":"crime","H3920":"to capture","H853":"[Obj.]","H7563":"wicked","H2256":"Mahalab","H2403":"sin","H1931":"he/she/it","H4191":"to die","H7230":"abundance","H200":"folly"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Thoughts. Or wisdom; and act with discretion. — Mind, &c., is omitted in Hebrew and St. Jerome. (Calmet) — By woman all concupiscence, or the inducement to sin, is commonly understood. We must not think of such things. (Worthington)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Sword. “It is a crime even to hearken.” (St. Ambrose, de Abrah. ii. 11.) She seeks thy ruin, ver. 5., and chap. ii. 16."],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","They. Hebrew, “if perhaps thou ponder the path of life.” (Pagnin) (Haydock) — Or “she ponders not,” &c. She walks inconsiderately, and consults only her passions, chap. vii. 10. (Calmet) — No one can depend on her love. (Menochius)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Strangers. The world, the flesh, and the devil are such; cruelly devising our ruin. (Worthington)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Strength. Or children, ver. 16., and Genesis xlix. 3. (Calmet)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Body. He alludes to a shameful disease, the just punishment of intemperance, Ecclesiasticus xix. 3."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Evil. Infirm and worn out, having lost my reputation, &c. (Calmet) — Though I lived among the faithful, I was under no restraint. (Menochius)"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Well. Live comfortably on your own property, (Cajetan) with your own wife. (Calmet)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Waters. Mayst thou have a numerous offspring, (ver. 10.) and be liberal. Many copies of the Septuagint, &c., have a negation, with Aquila, “let not thy,” &c., (Calmet) though it may be read with an interrogation, “are the waters of thy fountain to be?” &c. (De Dieu) — By no means. Origen (in Numbers xii.) acknowledges both readings. (Calmet) — Good instructions must be given to those who are well disposed, but not to scoffers, or obstinate infidels. (Worthington) — Husbands are exhorted to be content with their own wives, (ver. 15, 20.) so that the negative particle seems to be here wanting, as it is, chap. vi. 17., in Manuscript 60, (Kennicott) and chap. xiv. 33. (Septuagint, &c.) (Capellus)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Thee. Stick to thy own wife. In a moral sense, let those who instruct others, take care not to neglect themselves."],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Vein. Thou shalt have a numerous progeny, Psalm lxvii. 28., and Isaias xlviii. 1. (Calmet)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Love. This is spoken by way of permission, and to withdraw people from unlawful connections, Ecclesiastes ii. 1., and 1 Corinthians vii. 29. (Calmet)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Ropes. “Evil habits unrestrained induce a necessity,” (St. Augustine, Confessions viii. 5.) though not absolute. (Haydock) — The libertine thinks he can get free as soon as he pleases; not being aware of the chains which he is forging for himself. (Calmet) — Sin requires punishment. (Menochius)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}